WEBVTT

1
00:00:09.667 --> 00:00:15.281
Three days and three nights had gone by since the train had left San Francisco.

2
00:00:15.282 --> 00:00:22.771
The travelers had passed the Great Salt Lake in Utah, traveled over the Rocky Mountains, and crossed the prairies of Wyoming.

3
00:00:22.772 --> 00:00:30.529
In Nebraska they had reached the Platte River, which the rail line was now following, 350 miles before Omaha.

4
00:00:30.530 --> 00:00:36.756
Four more days and nights remained until they would reach New York. Phileas Fogg was right on schedule.

5
00:00:36.757 --> 00:00:43.252
Mr. Fogg and his friends continued their whist game, and no one complained about the length of the trip.

6
00:00:43.253 --> 00:00:49.034
During the morning Fix had won several guineas, which he now seemed likely to lose.

7
00:00:49.035 --> 00:00:52.197
The train pulled into the station at Plum Creek.

8
00:00:52.198 --> 00:00:59.649
Mr. Fogg was about to play a winning spade when a voice from behind him said, "A smart whist player would play a diamond!"

9
00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:01.574
It was Colonel Proctor.

10
00:01:01.575 --> 00:01:06.090
"I see I have found you, Englishman," said the colonel.

11
00:01:06.091 --> 00:01:10.084
Aouda turned pale. She seized Mr. Fogg's arm.

12
00:01:10.085 --> 00:01:15.336
But it was Mr. Fix who stood up and said, "Pardon me, but this is my affair.

13
00:01:15.337 --> 00:01:23.599
You hit me, Colonel, and now you are being insolent with one of my friends. I will challenge you myself!"

14
00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:28.065
"Mr. Fix, you will not," said Mr. Fogg, rising from his seat.

15
00:01:28.066 --> 00:01:33.406
"The colonel has insulted me a second time by suggesting that I play a diamond.

16
00:01:33.407 --> 00:01:36.603
I have no more patience for this rude American.

17
00:01:36.604 --> 00:01:39.450
I challenge you to a duel, Colonel!"

18
00:01:39.451 --> 00:01:45.873
"I accept your challenge," replied Colonel Proctor, "with whatever weapon you choose!"

19
00:01:45.874 --> 00:01:52.328
Passepartout wanted to beg Mr. Fogg not to proceed but knew that, as Fogg's servant, he must hold his tongue.

20
00:01:52.329 --> 00:01:56.814
Aouda, on the other hand, pleaded with Mr. Fogg not to risk his life.

21
00:01:56.815 --> 00:02:03.769
Mr. Fogg calmly selected four revolvers from Passepartout's collection and handed two to the colonel.

22
00:02:03.770 --> 00:02:08.022
He left the car, and the American followed him out onto the platform.

23
00:02:08.023 --> 00:02:12.397
The weather had turned cold and scattered snowflakes were drifting in the air.

24
00:02:12.398 --> 00:02:16.887
The conductor hurried to them to say the train was about to get underway again.

25
00:02:16.888 --> 00:02:22.666
"We are already twenty minutes late, gentlemen. Please board the train," he said.

26
00:02:22.667 --> 00:02:28.617
"We mean to fight a duel! And we would like to do it here and now," said Mr. Fogg.

27
00:02:28.618 --> 00:02:31.655
"I'm really very sorry," said the conductor.

28
00:02:31.656 --> 00:02:36.666
"Under any other circumstances, I'd be willing to oblige you, but there is no time.

29
00:02:36.667 --> 00:02:39.919
Why not fight as we go along?"

30
00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:47.127
The men reboarded the train. As it left the station, they followed the conductor to the last car.

31
00:02:47.128 --> 00:02:50.585
The conductor quickly relocated its few passengers.

32
00:02:50.586 --> 00:02:58.516
Mr. Fogg and Colonel Proctor, each carrying two six-barreled revolvers, faced each other at opposite ends of the empty car.

33
00:02:58.517 --> 00:03:02.404
They agreed to begin firing on the count of three.

34
00:03:02.405 --> 00:03:07.041
Fix and Passepartout, their hearts beating, waited outside the door.

35
00:03:07.042 --> 00:03:14.338
Suddenly they heard savage cries, accompanied by gunshots, but the shots did not come from the last car of the train.

36
00:03:14.339 --> 00:03:18.135
Shrieks of terror came from the passengers.

37
00:03:18.136 --> 00:03:26.022
Colonel Proctor and Mr. Fogg, revolvers in hand, left their dueling car and rushed to the forward cars with Fix and Passepartout.

38
00:03:26.023 --> 00:03:30.922
They saw right away what was causing the commotion.

39
00:03:30.923 --> 00:03:35.926
A hundred Indians had leaped from galloping horses onto the train.

40
00:03:35.927 --> 00:03:39.982
Three had climbed onto the engine and knocked the engineer unconscious.

41
00:03:39.983 --> 00:03:46.182
Now the train was racing forward at an uncontrollable speed. The Indians were armed with guns.

42
00:03:46.183 --> 00:03:50.771
They slipped through the windows and doors of the cars, shooting at the passengers.

43
00:03:50.772 --> 00:03:55.399
Many passengers were armed as well and fired back.

44
00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:58.459
The conductor was fighting beside Mr. Fogg.

45
00:03:58.460 --> 00:04:04.545
He knew that a garrison of soldiers was at the next station, Fort Kearney, which they were fast approaching.

46
00:04:04.546 --> 00:04:09.105
Their only hope was to stop the train so that the soldiers could come to their aid.

47
00:04:09.106 --> 00:04:12.315
A shot hit the conductor in the chest.

48
00:04:12.316 --> 00:04:18.995
As he fell, he cried, "Unless the train is stopped, we are lost!"

49
00:04:18.996 --> 00:04:24.320
"It shall be stopped," said Phileas Fogg, preparing to rush through the cars to the engine.

50
00:04:24.321 --> 00:04:29.329
"Stay, monsieur, and protect Aouda," said Passepartout. "I will go!"

51
00:04:29.330 --> 00:04:37.449
Mr. Fogg had no time to stop the courageous Passepartout, who crawled through a door and slipped under the train!

52
00:04:37.450 --> 00:04:42.482
While bullets whizzed between the Indians and the passengers, smashing windows and puncturing seats,

53
00:04:42.483 --> 00:04:46.495
Passepartout clung dangerously above the speeding tracks.

54
00:04:46.496 --> 00:04:51.472
He crawled forward from one car to the next until he reached the first car.

55
00:04:51.473 --> 00:04:58.104
Hanging by one hand, he released the chains that attached the engine to the rest of the train.

56
00:04:58.105 --> 00:05:03.457
The train shuddered violently, and the engine detached from the rest of the cars.

57
00:05:03.458 --> 00:05:07.570
The engine raced ahead, but the cars began to slow.

58
00:05:07.571 --> 00:05:13.363
After several minutes they stopped, only a hundred feet from the station.

59
00:05:13.364 --> 00:05:16.803
Passepartout crawled out from underneath the first car.

60
00:05:16.804 --> 00:05:20.989
Before he could brush himself off, he felt a pistol against his head.

61
00:05:20.990 --> 00:05:29.874
He turned to see a group of Indians had surrounded him on horseback, and everything went black.

62
00:05:29.875 --> 00:05:32.703
Help! Help! Somebody, help me!

63
00:05:32.704 --> 00:05:37.530
Oh, hello there. As you can see, I'm in a bit of trouble, captured by Indians!

64
00:05:37.531 --> 00:05:40.496
Let's hope Mr. Fogg comes to my rescue soon.

65
00:05:40.497 --> 00:05:45.105
At least he didn't get killed in that duel. What was he thinking?

66
00:05:45.106 --> 00:05:50.002
Perhaps you have realized this on your own, but a duel is a confrontation between two men.

67
00:05:50.003 --> 00:05:54.783
A duel is usually arranged when one man is insulted or wronged by the other man.

68
00:05:54.784 --> 00:06:00.673
For example, Mr. Fogg said that Colonel Proctor had insulted him twice, so he challenged Proctor to a duel.

69
00:06:00.674 --> 00:06:04.005
It was a way of regaining his honor.

70
00:06:04.006 --> 00:06:08.451
Duels are usually fought with guns and are common in Europe and the United States.

71
00:06:08.452 --> 00:06:14.211
They frequently take place at dawn so the men will not be interrupted by townspeople or police.

72
00:06:14.212 --> 00:06:21.554
Duels are also common among cowboys in the "Wild West" of America, especially when a new cowboy comes to town.

73
00:06:21.555 --> 00:06:27.439
For a duel with guns, two men stand back-to-back, and then walk forward a certain number of steps.

74
00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:31.468
After that, they quickly turn to face each other and shoot.

75
00:06:31.469 --> 00:06:37.581
It's a dangerous way to settle an argument!
